I mean it, there might be spoilers here.

But then again, why would you be reading this newsgroup if you weren't
hungry for spoilers?

So read at your own risk.

I didn't see this mentioned in the spoiler sheet I perused a while 
back, and since then, I haven't noticed anything about it, so I am 
assuming that I'm not posting common knowledge.  I haven't been 
religiously reading this group, though, since I've already learned
as much as I've needed.  And besides, I've quit.  Really I have.  And
I wasn't addicted in the first place anyway.  I just chose to play for
25 hours straight, three times in a week.  Really.

In the spoiler sheet, it mentioned that if you engraved with several
wands, they would identify themselves to you, but there are some wands
which it incorrectly says can't be identified by this method.  The
trick is, if you engrave on a space which already has engraving
on it, they make their presence known.  Stop reading if you don't want
more precise information, and just want to find out on your own.









The wands that can be identified in this fashion are the wands of 
polymorph, cancellation, make invisible, and teleportation.  I once
thought that one of the last three was identifiable without previous
engraving, but I just checked in explorer mode, patch level 10, and
it didn't appear so.  If you engrave with any of the last three wands
on a square that has writing on it, you get the message "the engraving
vanishes" or some such.  It is the same message for all three, so you
only get partial id, but it's better than nothing.  If you engrave with
a wand of teleportation, something else happens.  Somewhere on the level
you're on, the engraving you were standing on will resurface.  Since 
what I usually engrave, through force of habit, is Elbereth, this often
gives me the impression that I've found a ghost level, and I move very
carefully through a harmless level.

The polymorph wand does something different every time you use it.  It 
polymorphs the writing!  It gives you the message "(something) is now
scribbled on the rock" or whatever, with random messages in lieu of 
something.  It might be ad aerum est, or Elbereth, or my personal favorite,
"You won't get it up the steps."  If this feature is new to you, play
around with it and amuse and delight your friends.

Now, for the real, hard core spoilers:  my personal wand id strategy.

First, I write in the dust with my finger, usually Elbereth, although
anything will do.  It's important not to engrave with an athame or other
weapon, or a wand of fire or lightning, because most wands need to be
engraved with to identify, and you'll get the message that you can't 
erase the message that's already there, and, I think, waste a charge.
If it's any of the four mentioned above, I know mostly what it is, and 
if not, I'll engrave with it and hopefully identify it.  If I know that I
have one of the three "vanishes" wands, and if I have something that's 
cursed and not particularly useful, I'll drop it, move back a step, and
zap it.  If it was teleportation, the item will disappear.  If it was
cancellation, the item will be uncursed, and if it was make invisible,
nothing will happen.  Any way, I'll have identified the wand with just 
two charges.  If I don't have anything useless and cursed (or +- something)
then I try to find an immobile, unthreatening monster (Fungi are best, 
priests/priestesses are worst) and zap it.  Now, if it's cancellation,
nothing will have happened, and I can kill the thing and name my wand.
If it was make invisible, when I try to step on the space, it will say
"you hit the invisible yellow mold" and I can kill it and name my wand.
If it was teleportation, when I go to step on the space, nothing will be
there to greet me.  This is why immobile nasties are best, because you
know where they are even if you can't see them.  

Of course, if you can see invisible, this won't work so well.

Well, enough for this post.  See you in hell (or at least your armor)

